\details{
\strong{1. Plot-time annotation subsetting}

Syn-aware annotation geoms subset lazily at build time by combining
\verb{chr =} with \code{subset = c(start, end)}. This is the most common plotting
workflow when you want to display only one genomic window from a larger
GFF/GTF file.

\preformatted{
ggexon(sp) +
  geom_exon(
    species = "XZ1516",
    chr = "RagTag_V",
    subset = c(21558028, 21620381)
  )
}

The whole annotation file stays attached to the \code{SynIndividual}; only the
requested window is resolved for the layer.

\strong{2. Direct feature queries}

Use \code{\link[=query_features]{query_features()}} when you want the subsetted annotation ranges as a
\code{GRanges} object instead of a plot layer. This is useful for inspection,
downstream computation, or building a custom plotting table.

\preformatted{
ind <- individuals(sp)[["XZ1516"]]
gr <- query_features(
  ind,
  chr = "RagTag_V",
  start = 21558028,
  end = 21620381,
  feature_type = "exon"
)
}

\strong{3. Object-level window subsetting}

Use \code{\link[=subset_feature_annotation]{subset_feature_annotation()}} or \code{\link[=subset_individual]{subset_individual()}} when you want a
reusable windowed object rather than a one-off \code{GRanges} query. These
helpers return trimmed snapshots that are ready to pass back into \code{ggexon()}
or to inspect directly.

\preformatted{
ind <- individuals(sp)[["XZ1516"]]
small_ind <- subset_individual(
  ind,
  chr = "RagTag_V",
  start = 21558028,
  end = 21620381
)
}

\strong{4. Comparative window subsetting}

Use \code{\link[=subset_synspecies_window]{subset_synspecies_window()}} when you want one reference window and the
corresponding linked window on the partner genome. This helper returns both
annotation subsets and the retained link rows.

\preformatted{
out <- subset_synspecies_window(
  sp,
  reference_species = "XZ1516",
  chr = "RagTag_V",
  start = 21574445,
  end = 21584356,
  alignment = "XZ1516_vs_N2"
)
}

The return value is a list with:
